1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,660
Here is a square turning into a circle.

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:05,280
It takes about three seconds,
and it looks effortless.

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:07,460
But a screen cannot show motion.

4
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:09,120
It can only show still pictures,

5
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:13,240
one after another, fast enough
that your brain fills in the gaps.

6
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:16,440
So this little clip is really
a stack of still frames.

7
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:18,640
Dozens of them at preview quality,

8
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:21,940
close to two hundred at the
smoothness of a finished video.

9
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:24,440
And each frame is a
slightly different shape:

10
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:26,540
not quite a square, not yet a circle.

11
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:30,160
Here is the question that this
whole series grows out of.

12
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:33,800
Who drew all of those in-between shapes?

13
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:35,360
The traditional answer is:

14
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,780
an artist did, or an artist
drew the start and the end,

15
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:40,640
and software guessed the middle.

16
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:45,460
That is called key framing, and it works
wonderfully for characters and logos.

17
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:48,160
But think about what a
mathematician wants to animate.

18
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:51,640
A curve that is exactly
the graph of a function.

19
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:55,220
A triangle whose corners
stay exactly on a circle.

20
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:59,420
An equation whose terms slide
to exactly the right places.

21
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:02,840
Guessing the middle is not good enough
when the middle is the whole point.

22
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:04,640
So here is a different idea.

23
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:07,620
Instead of drawing frames,
describe the change.

24
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:09,180
Make a square. Draw it.

25
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:10,680
Turn it into a circle.

26
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:11,620
Pause for a moment.

27
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:13,940
If those sentences are precise enough,

28
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:17,300
a computer can work out every
in-between frame for you.

29
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:19,840
For any moment in time, it asks:

30
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:21,360
how far along are we?

31
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:22,460
A quarter of the way?

32
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:26,580
Then compute the shape that is a quarter
of the way from square to circle.

33
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:28,720
Halfway? Compute that one.

34
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:32,540
And a description precise enough for
a computer to follow has another name.

35
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:35,383
It is a program.

36
00:01:35,383 --> 00:01:38,103
That is exactly the idea behind Manim.

37
00:01:38,103 --> 00:01:40,483
The name is short for math animation.

38
00:01:40,483 --> 00:01:41,963
It was written by Grant Sanderson,

39
00:01:41,963 --> 00:01:45,883
around 2015, to make the
videos on his channel,

40
00:01:45,883 --> 00:01:48,683
and he shared the code so
that anyone could use it.

41
00:01:48,683 --> 00:01:50,403
As more people began using it,

42
00:01:50,403 --> 00:01:52,983
a group of them formed Manim Community,

43
00:01:52,983 --> 00:01:56,483
a separately maintained
edition focused on stability,

44
00:01:56,483 --> 00:01:58,943
documentation and new features.

45
00:01:58,943 --> 00:02:01,023
The two have drifted apart in places,

46
00:02:01,023 --> 00:02:02,903
so when this series says Manim,

47
00:02:02,903 --> 00:02:06,443
it means the Community
edition and its conventions.

48
00:02:06,443 --> 00:02:09,543
Normally, using Manim
means installing Python,

49
00:02:09,543 --> 00:02:13,143
the library, and a handful of
supporting tools for drawing,

50
00:02:13,143 --> 00:02:15,103
video encoding and typesetting.

51
00:02:15,103 --> 00:02:17,823
That is a real hurdle on your first day.

52
00:02:17,823 --> 00:02:20,803
So in this series we will
mostly work somewhere else:

53
00:02:20,803 --> 00:02:23,663
ManimOnline. It is a website,

54
00:02:23,663 --> 00:02:28,423
and it runs a Manim-compatible engine
entirely inside your browser tab.

55
00:02:28,423 --> 00:02:29,603
Nothing to install.

56
00:02:29,603 --> 00:02:31,563
And as we will see much later,

57
00:02:31,563 --> 00:02:34,217
nothing running on a server either.

58
00:02:34,217 --> 00:02:36,817
Let's open it. On the
left is a code editor.

59
00:02:36,817 --> 00:02:38,517
On the right is the preview.

60
00:02:38,517 --> 00:02:42,557
Along the top, a toolbar we will
explore over the course of the series.

61
00:02:42,557 --> 00:02:45,997
The first time you visit, the page
downloads a Python interpreter,

62
00:02:45,997 --> 00:02:48,437
so the very first render
takes a little while.

63
00:02:48,437 --> 00:02:50,637
After that, things move quickly.

64
00:02:50,637 --> 00:02:52,277
If you would like to follow along,

65
00:02:52,277 --> 00:02:53,677
scan the code in the corner.

66
00:02:53,677 --> 00:02:56,297
It opens this exact scene in your browser.

67
00:02:56,297 --> 00:02:59,237
Once it renders, the preview
behaves like a video player.

68
00:02:59,237 --> 00:03:03,057
You can play, pause, and drag the
seek bar to scrub through time.

69
00:03:03,057 --> 00:03:05,997
That last part is more
interesting than it looks.

70
00:03:05,997 --> 00:03:09,377
The page already computed every
frame before it started playing,

71
00:03:09,377 --> 00:03:11,957
so jumping to any moment is instant.

72
00:03:11,957 --> 00:03:13,137
Two more things worth knowing.

73
00:03:13,137 --> 00:03:16,937
ManimOnline also understands
two diagram languages,

74
00:03:16,937 --> 00:03:21,537
Graphviz and modl, and it guesses
which language you are writing.

75
00:03:21,537 --> 00:03:23,237
The Renderer selector says Auto,

76
00:03:23,237 --> 00:03:26,517
and for Python scenes it
will choose Manim on its own.

77
00:03:26,517 --> 00:03:30,467
And the Sample gallery link opens a
collection of ready-made examples,

78
00:03:30,467 --> 00:03:33,557
which is a wonderful
place to steal ideas from.

79
00:03:33,557 --> 00:03:36,867
We will come back to both in episode nine.

80
00:03:36,867 --> 00:03:39,487
Let's read this program
one line at a time.

81
00:03:39,487 --> 00:03:40,087
It is short,

82
00:03:40,087 --> 00:03:44,267
but every line is doing something you
will use in every scene you ever write.

83
00:03:44,267 --> 00:03:45,627
The first line says:

84
00:03:45,627 --> 00:03:47,547
from manim, import everything.

85
00:03:47,547 --> 00:03:49,667
That fills your toolbox with names.

86
00:03:49,667 --> 00:03:51,487
Shapes like Square and Circle,

87
00:03:51,487 --> 00:03:54,027
animations like Create, colours like BLUE,

88
00:03:54,027 --> 00:03:55,627
and directions like UP.

89
00:03:55,627 --> 00:03:58,247
Next, a class called SquareToCircle,

90
00:03:58,247 --> 00:03:59,767
which is a kind of Scene.

91
00:03:59,767 --> 00:04:01,847
If you have not written classes before,

92
00:04:01,847 --> 00:04:04,967
do not worry. For now,
read it as a template:

93
00:04:04,967 --> 00:04:08,207
"Here is a scene, and this is its name."

94
00:04:08,207 --> 00:04:10,367
A single file can hold several scenes,

95
00:04:10,367 --> 00:04:12,687
and the name is how you
choose between them.

96
00:04:12,687 --> 00:04:15,827
Inside the scene is a
method called construct.

97
00:04:15,827 --> 00:04:18,847
When the scene is rendered,
Manim calls construct,

98
00:04:18,847 --> 00:04:21,327
and everything inside it happens in order,

99
00:04:21,327 --> 00:04:23,147
from top to bottom.

100
00:04:23,147 --> 00:04:26,447
You can think of construct as
the script for this little film.

101
00:04:26,447 --> 00:04:29,507
The first two lines inside make objects.

102
00:04:29,507 --> 00:04:32,067
A blue square and a yellow circle.

103
00:04:32,067 --> 00:04:33,787
And here is a subtle point.

104
00:04:33,787 --> 00:04:37,007
Creating an object does
not put it on screen.

105
00:04:37,007 --> 00:04:40,407
Right now, both shapes exist
in the computer's memory,

106
00:04:40,407 --> 00:04:41,887
waiting in the wings.

107
00:04:41,887 --> 00:04:45,327
Then: self dot play, Create, square.

108
00:04:45,327 --> 00:04:47,947
This is the first line that takes time.

109
00:04:47,947 --> 00:04:49,767
Play means: run this animation,

110
00:04:49,767 --> 00:04:51,787
and record the frames.

111
00:04:51,787 --> 00:04:53,567
Create is a particular animation,

112
00:04:53,567 --> 00:04:56,947
one that draws an object's outline
as if a pen were tracing it.

113
00:04:56,947 --> 00:05:00,287
And when it finishes,
the square is on stage.

114
00:05:00,287 --> 00:05:02,787
Next: self dot play, Transform,

115
00:05:02,787 --> 00:05:04,387
square into circle.

116
00:05:04,387 --> 00:05:07,467
The square smoothly becomes
the shape of the circle.

117
00:05:07,467 --> 00:05:09,207
Keep an eye on the wording there.

118
00:05:09,207 --> 00:05:10,907
The square becomes circle-shaped;

119
00:05:10,907 --> 00:05:13,387
the circle object we
made is used as a target,

120
00:05:13,387 --> 00:05:15,107
a picture of where to end up.

121
00:05:15,107 --> 00:05:19,227
We will find out in episode three why
that distinction matters more than it

122
00:05:19,227 --> 00:05:20,027
seems.

123
00:05:20,027 --> 00:05:21,767
And finally, self dot wait.

124
00:05:21,767 --> 00:05:24,627
Nothing moves. The scene
simply holds for a second,

125
00:05:24,627 --> 00:05:28,367
so the viewer has a moment to
look at what just happened.

126
00:05:28,367 --> 00:05:31,067
If you squint, that program
has a small grammar,

127
00:05:31,067 --> 00:05:32,987
and it is the grammar of all of Manim.

128
00:05:32,987 --> 00:05:36,087
There is the scene, the stage
where everything happens.

129
00:05:36,087 --> 00:05:39,307
There are mobjects, short
for mathematical objects:

130
00:05:39,307 --> 00:05:40,927
anything you can see, like shapes,

131
00:05:40,927 --> 00:05:42,307
text, graphs and arrows.

132
00:05:42,307 --> 00:05:46,707
And there are animations, which
describe how mobjects change over time.

133
00:05:46,707 --> 00:05:48,987
Then there is the verb
that ties them together:

134
00:05:48,987 --> 00:05:52,467
play. Play takes animations,
works out every in-between moment,

135
00:05:52,467 --> 00:05:54,147
and turns them into frames.

136
00:05:54,147 --> 00:05:56,047
Episode two is all about mobjects.

137
00:05:56,047 --> 00:05:57,467
Episode three is all about animations.

138
00:05:57,467 --> 00:06:01,317
But you already know enough
to start changing things.

139
00:06:01,317 --> 00:06:04,837
The best way to learn this is to
change things and see what breaks.

140
00:06:04,837 --> 00:06:06,457
Try giving the square a fill.

141
00:06:06,457 --> 00:06:10,597
Add fill opacity equals zero
point five inside the parentheses.

142
00:06:10,597 --> 00:06:15,057
Stop typing, and after a short pause
the preview renders again by itself.

143
00:06:15,057 --> 00:06:16,997
Now add another transform:

144
00:06:16,997 --> 00:06:19,277
turn the shape into a red triangle.

145
00:06:19,277 --> 00:06:21,417
The code in the corner
has all three shapes,

146
00:06:21,417 --> 00:06:24,037
and a comment inviting
you to add a fourth,

147
00:06:24,037 --> 00:06:25,397
a green star.

148
00:06:25,397 --> 00:06:28,337
Notice that the new version gave
the shape a more general name,

149
00:06:28,337 --> 00:06:33,337
shape instead of square, because it is
not going to stay a square for long.

150
00:06:33,337 --> 00:06:35,977
Names are for you, not for Manim.

151
00:06:35,977 --> 00:06:39,717
Choose the one that will still
be true at the end of the scene.

152
00:06:39,717 --> 00:06:41,215
Here is something worth noticing.

153
00:06:41,215 --> 00:06:44,268
In a Manim scene, time
does not pass on its own.

154
00:06:44,268 --> 00:06:46,021
It only passes when you say so.

155
00:06:46,021 --> 00:06:49,829
Self dot add puts an
object on stage instantly,

156
00:06:49,829 --> 00:06:52,314
with no animation, and
takes no time at all.

157
00:06:52,314 --> 00:06:54,938
Each call to play adds the
length of its animation,

158
00:06:54,938 --> 00:06:57,608
which is one second
unless you say otherwise.

159
00:06:57,608 --> 00:06:59,918
Wait adds however many
seconds you ask for,

160
00:06:59,918 --> 00:07:02,078
and one if you do not say.

161
00:07:02,078 --> 00:07:04,829
To make an animation
slower, pass run time.

162
00:07:04,829 --> 00:07:08,521
Run time equals three makes the
second slide take three seconds.

163
00:07:08,521 --> 00:07:11,818
Add it all up and this scene
lasts five and a half seconds,

164
00:07:11,818 --> 00:07:14,756
and you could have predicted
that before it ever rendered.

165
00:07:14,756 --> 00:07:17,356
That is a surprisingly powerful habit.

166
00:07:17,356 --> 00:07:20,595
When a scene feels rushed, you
know exactly which line to stretch.

167
00:07:20,595 --> 00:07:22,987
When it drags, you know
which wait to shorten.

168
00:07:22,987 --> 00:07:24,960
And when you later add a voice-over,

169
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:26,899
like the one you are listening to now,

170
00:07:26,899 --> 00:07:30,057
you can match every animation
to the length of a sentence.

171
00:07:30,057 --> 00:07:32,101
This voice-over, by the way, is synthetic:

172
00:07:32,101 --> 00:07:35,920
text-to-speech built from a
model of Curt Cox's voice.

173
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:40,576
It reads names from code and terms
from math as ordinary English words,

174
00:07:40,576 --> 00:07:42,956
so you may hear some of
them, like construct,

175
00:07:42,956 --> 00:07:47,467
pronounced differently than programmers
or mathematicians would say them.

176
00:07:47,467 --> 00:07:49,940
Sooner or later, you will make a mistake.

177
00:07:49,940 --> 00:07:52,215
Everyone does, constantly,
and that is fine.

178
00:07:52,215 --> 00:07:55,118
What matters is reading
what the computer tells you.

179
00:07:55,118 --> 00:07:58,659
Scan this code and you will get
an error instead of an animation.

180
00:07:58,659 --> 00:08:00,470
The last line says NameError:

181
00:08:00,470 --> 00:08:04,510
name C I R C E L is not defined.

182
00:08:04,510 --> 00:08:05,659
Did you mean Circle?

183
00:08:05,659 --> 00:08:08,864
Python is telling you it has never
heard of anything called Circel,

184
00:08:08,864 --> 00:08:11,070
and it even suggests
what you probably meant.

185
00:08:11,070 --> 00:08:13,380
The line just above it says where:

186
00:08:13,380 --> 00:08:15,330
line seven, inside construct.

187
00:08:15,330 --> 00:08:17,629
Find that word in the
code, fix the spelling,

188
00:08:17,629 --> 00:08:19,940
and stop typing. The scene renders again,

189
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:21,507
and this time it works.

190
00:08:21,507 --> 00:08:23,330
Most early errors are like this one:

191
00:08:23,330 --> 00:08:25,651
a misspelled name, a missing parenthesis,

192
00:08:25,651 --> 00:08:27,509
or forgetting to import something.

193
00:08:27,509 --> 00:08:29,506
Read the last line of the message first.

194
00:08:29,506 --> 00:08:33,817
It usually names the problem,
and very often the exact spot.

195
00:08:33,817 --> 00:08:36,737
Let's take stock. An animation
is a stack of frames.

196
00:08:36,737 --> 00:08:38,917
Instead of drawing them,
you describe the change,

197
00:08:38,917 --> 00:08:40,617
and that description is a program.

198
00:08:40,617 --> 00:08:43,337
In Manim, a scene's construct
method is that description.

199
00:08:43,337 --> 00:08:45,417
Mobjects are the things you see,

200
00:08:45,417 --> 00:08:46,897
animations are how they change,

201
00:08:46,897 --> 00:08:48,917
and play turns animations into frames,

202
00:08:48,917 --> 00:08:50,657
adding time only when you ask.

203
00:08:50,657 --> 00:08:52,017
Here is a small challenge.

204
00:08:52,017 --> 00:08:53,737
This last code builds a traffic light,

205
00:08:53,737 --> 00:08:55,037
and lights the green lamp.

206
00:08:55,037 --> 00:08:56,657
Your job is to finish the cycle:

207
00:08:56,657 --> 00:08:58,197
switch green off, light yellow,

208
00:08:58,197 --> 00:08:59,197
wait, then light red.

209
00:08:59,197 --> 00:09:00,477
There is a hint in the comments,

210
00:09:00,477 --> 00:09:02,937
and everything you need is something
you have already seen today.

211
00:09:02,937 --> 00:09:05,177
Next time, we zoom in on the nouns:

212
00:09:05,177 --> 00:09:07,277
where things go on
screen, how to style them,

213
00:09:07,277 --> 00:09:08,277
and how to group them,

214
00:09:08,277 --> 00:09:11,352
plus one trap that catches almost
everyone in their first week.
